# The image is the control plane and nothing else. Everything below either
# belongs to another artifact or would put a secret, a credential, or a
# hundred megabytes of Go into a container that has no use for it.
.git
.github
engine
runner
tools
docs
examples
schemas
bin
infra
# The marketing site. Six hundred megabytes of it, and the reason this line
# exists: it was missing, so every control plane build tarred up www and its
# node_modules and streamed them to the daemon. The build worked. It took
# thirty minutes instead of forty seconds and nothing said why.
#
# The lesson is the shape of this file rather than the one directory. A list
# of what to leave out is only correct until somebody adds a directory, and
# nothing tells you the day that happens. `af up` now prints the size of a
# context this large, which is the check that would have caught it.
www
deploy
# The Dockerfiles themselves have to survive this, and it is easy to miss why.
# `docker build -f` reads a Dockerfile from the host and never consults the
# ignore file, so excluding it here still builds by hand. `af up` sends the
# context to the daemon and names the Dockerfile *inside* it, so an excluded
# one is simply not there, and the daemon answers "cannot locate specified
# Dockerfile" about a file that is plainly on disk.
!deploy/docker/app.Dockerfile
!deploy/docker/control-plane.Dockerfile
!deploy/docker/bootstrap.mjs
!deploy/docker/maintenance.mjs
!deploy/docker/personas.mjs
ee
web/node_modules
web/**/node_modules
# A local build's output. Sending it wastes a hundred megabytes on every build
# and the image would ignore it anyway: the build stage produces its own.
web/**/.next
web/**/test
**/*.test.ts
# Working directories, none of which exist on a clean clone and all of which
# are large on a machine somebody has been building on.
.antifailure
.gate-reports
.playwright-mcp
**/*.broken.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
*.md
# The manifest. Neither image reads it, and leaving it in the context makes
# every image's tag depend on it: the tag is derived from the context digest,
# so editing a workflow expectation or an egress rule changes the digest and
# asks the daemon for an image that has never been built. The layers still
# cache, and the rebuild is still work nobody asked for.
antifailure.yaml
masking.yaml
